  RK.add({ track: TR, id: '1.2', dom: 1, short: 'OUs, groups & lifecycle', title: 'Design OUs and groups and manage the account lifecycle',
    goal: 'Use OUs for policy and delegation, choose group type and scope (AGDLP), know the privileged groups, and run joiner/mover/leaver correctly.',
    kw: /\b(organizational unit|\bou\b|group scope|global group|domain local|universal|distribution group|security group|agdlp|domain admins|enterprise admins|schema admins|protected users|account lifecycle|joiner|leaver|disabled|expired)/,
    parts: [
      P('OUs and group scopes', 'Use OUs for policy and delegation and choose the right group type and scope.',
        "<h3>Organizational Units (OUs)</h3><p>An <b>OU</b> is a folder inside a domain. You create OUs to (1) <b>link Group Policy</b> to a set of objects and (2) <b>delegate</b> administration (for example let the helpdesk reset passwords only in the Sales OU).</p><ul><li><b>OU vs group:</b> an OU is a <i>location</i> (an object lives in exactly one OU); a group is a <i>collection</i> (a user can be in many). <b>Group Policy is applied to OUs, not groups</b> (groups can only filter it). <b>Permissions are given to groups, not OUs.</b></li><li>The default <code>CN=Users</code> and <code>CN=Computers</code> are <b>containers</b>, not OUs — you cannot link a GPO to them. Create your own OUs and move objects out.</li><li>Turn on <b>Protect from accidental deletion</b>. New OUs created with PowerShell are protected by default.</li><li>Design OUs around <b>how you manage</b> things (Workstations, Servers, Staff by department), not around the org chart.</li></ul><h3>Group types and scopes</h3><p><b>Type:</b> <b>Security</b> groups get permissions and can be used in ACLs. <b>Distribution</b> groups only send email (no permissions).</p><table><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Members can come from</th><th>Can be used to grant permissions on</th></tr><tr><td><b>Domain Local</b></td><td>Any domain in the forest</td><td>Resources in <b>its own domain</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Global</b></td><td><b>Its own domain</b> only</td><td>Resources in any domain in the forest</td></tr><tr><td><b>Universal</b></td><td>Any domain in the forest</td><td>Anywhere (stored in the Global Catalog; changes replicate forest-wide)</td></tr></table><p><b>Best-practice recipe — AGDLP:</b> <b>A</b>ccounts go in <b>G</b>lobal groups (organized by role: <code>G-Finance</code>); Global groups go in <b>D</b>omain <b>L</b>ocal groups (organized by resource access: <code>DL-FinanceShare-RW</code>); the Domain Local group gets the <b>P</b>ermission on the resource. To give a new hire access, you only add them to one Global group.</p>" + pro('Design OUs around how you manage things (GPOs and delegation), not around the org chart — departments change every year, management needs rarely do.') + trap('Moving a user to an OU changes which GPOs and delegations apply to them; adding them to a group changes what they can access. Do not mix the two up.'),
        [['What are OUs mainly used for?', 'Linking GPOs and delegating administration', ['Granting share permissions', 'Sending email', 'Replication'], 'Location.'],
         ['In AGDLP, which group gets the permission on the resource?', 'Domain Local', ['Global', 'Universal', 'Distribution'], 'DL → permission.']],
        'adx-scope'),
      P('Privileged groups and the account lifecycle', 'Know the privileged built-in groups and run joiner, mover and leaver processes.',
        "<h3>Privileged built-in groups</h3><ul><li><b>Domain Admins</b> — full control of the domain. Member of every PC’s local Administrators group.</li><li><b>Enterprise Admins</b> — full control of the forest; exists only in the forest root domain. <b>Should be empty</b> day-to-day.</li><li><b>Schema Admins</b> — can modify the schema. <b>Should be empty</b> except during a change.</li><li><b>Administrators</b> (built-in) — local to DCs, includes Domain/Enterprise Admins.</li><li><b>Account Operators, Server Operators, Backup Operators, Print Operators</b> — legacy partial-admin groups; <b>Backup Operators</b> can copy NTDS.dit, so treat them as Tier 0. Keep them empty if you can.</li><li><b>Domain Users</b> — every user account. <b>Authenticated Users</b> — any identity that logged on (a special identity, not a group you manage).</li><li><b>Protected Users</b> — hardening group: no NTLM, no delegation, no cached credentials, short Kerberos TGT lifetimes. Use for admins.</li></ul><h3>Account states and the user lifecycle</h3><table><tr><th>State</th><th>What it means</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td><b>Disabled</b></td><td>An admin turned it off</td><td><code>Enable-ADAccount</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>Locked out</b></td><td>Too many bad passwords (policy), clears automatically after the lockout duration</td><td><code>Unlock-ADAccount</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>Account expired</b></td><td>Expiration date passed (contractors)</td><td>Extend the date</td></tr><tr><td><b>Password expired</b></td><td>Older than max password age</td><td>User changes it / admin resets it</td></tr></table><p><b>Joiner → Mover → Leaver (JML):</b> <b>Joiner</b>: create the account in the right OU, add to role groups, set a temporary password with “must change at next logon.” <b>Mover</b>: swap role groups (remove old, add new). <b>Leaver</b>: <b>disable first</b>, move to a Disabled OU, strip privileged groups, note the date in the description, and <b>delete only after the retention period</b> — deleting immediately destroys the SID and any chance to recover files or mailbox.</p>" + pro('Movers are the forgotten case: when someone changes department, remove the old access the same day. "Access creep" is how interns end up with payroll rights.') + trap('Disabled (admin action) and locked out (too many bad passwords) are different states with different fixes: Enable-ADAccount vs Unlock-ADAccount.'),
        [['What should you do first when an employee leaves?', 'Disable the account', ['Delete the account immediately', 'Rename it', 'Move it to Domain Admins'], 'Disable first.'],
         ['Which group should normally be empty day to day?', 'Schema Admins', ['Domain Users', 'Remote Desktop Users', 'Print Operators only'], 'Add only when needed.']],
        'adx-leaver')
    ],
    wrap: { recap: ['OU = location for GPO links and delegation; group = access.', 'Security vs distribution groups; Domain Local, Global, Universal scopes.', 'AGDLP: Accounts → Global (role) → Domain Local (resource) → Permission.', 'Privileged: Domain Admins, Enterprise/Schema Admins (empty), Administrators, Account/Backup Operators.', 'States: disabled, locked out, expired, password expired.', 'Leaver: disable → revoke → strip → move/annotate → delete after retention.'], lab: 'adx-ou-ps' }
  });

